# Copy to docker/.env — which is gitignored, and must stay that way.
#
#   cp docker/.env.example docker/.env
#
# The token is the one thing in this project that must never reach the repo,
# an image layer, or a company's world.

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The token. Pick ONE of the two ways below.
#
# Get one with: claude setup-token
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------

# PREFERRED — a command that PRINTS the token. docker/up.sh runs it at launch
# and hands the result to compose through the environment, so no secret is
# ever written to disk. Start the stack with:
#
#   docker/up.sh up --build
#
# KeePassXC (macOS; the CLI lives inside the app bundle and is not on PATH).
# It will ask for your database passphrase on the terminal.
# HELMSTED_TOKEN_CMD="/Applications/KeePassXC.app/Contents/MacOS/keepassxc-cli show -s -a Password ~/Vaults/personal.kdbx 'Claude Code'"
#
# KeePassXC (Linux, or Homebrew's keepassxc-cli on PATH)
# HELMSTED_TOKEN_CMD="keepassxc-cli show -s -a Password ~/vaults/personal.kdbx 'Claude Code'"
#
# macOS Keychain — no passphrase prompt after the first approval:
#   security add-generic-password -s helmsted -a claude -w
# HELMSTED_TOKEN_CMD="security find-generic-password -s helmsted -a claude -w"
#
# 1Password
# HELMSTED_TOKEN_CMD="op read op://Private/Claude Code/credential"
#
# pass
# HELMSTED_TOKEN_CMD="pass show helmsted/claude-token"

# FALLBACK — the token itself, in plaintext, right here. Works with a bare
# `docker compose -f docker/compose.yaml up`, and is a long-lived credential
# sitting in a source tree. Fine for a throwaway machine; not for yours.
CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN=

# Where companies live on the HOST. A bind mount, so every company is ordinary
# files on your disk whether the container is running or not — and it is what
# docker/backup.sh copies. Defaults to ~/.helmsted.
# HELMSTED_DATA=/Users/you/.helmsted

# Who the first company is, if the installation is empty when it boots. Once a
# company exists these do nothing; renaming one is a console action, because
# changing a name mid-life orphans everything already attributed to it.
# HELMSTED_COMPANY=Untitled Company
# HELMSTED_BUSINESS=
# HELMSTED_CEO=CEO
# HELMSTED_CHAIR=Chair

# The console, published on loopback only.
# PORT=4173

# Ownership of the files written into the mount. Docker Desktop maps this for
# you; on rootful Linux it does not, and a mismatch is what makes /data
# unwritable. The entrypoint says so plainly rather than failing later.
# UID=1000
# GID=1000

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NOT set here, on purpose:
#
#   Which hosts the staff may reach   docker/proxy/allowlist.conf
#     One host per line, subdomains need their own entry. It is compiled into
#     anchored regexes at BUILD time, so the running proxy image is read-only
#     and the list cannot be edited from inside the box. Rebuild to change it.
#
#   The daily spend cap               src/policy/rules.ts (dailyCapCents)
#     Part of the company's constitution rather than its environment, because
#     it is one of the six rules the staff are told about and can quote back.
